    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Benelli Showed the World Its New Tornado Naked Tre 903 Concept at EICMA 2026 and It's a Triple!\nAlia CTOL Flight Seals BETA’s Partnership with Sinclair Community College\n8 photos\nPhoto: Sinclair Community College\nBETA is, without a doubt, one of the most successful electric aircraft maker in the global market right now, and one of its most recent partnership proves that this success extends beyond commercial and defense applications, to the academic environment as well. It’s not just about making eVTOLs, but also teaching the next generations how to better integrate them.\nThis has been a very busy and life-changing year for Beta Technologies, with its CTOL (the conventional takeoff and landing version of its flagship electric aircraft) landing at the center of historic aviation operations in New Zealand, the UK, and back home in the US, through its dazzling participation in the eIPP\n.\nAfter all of these milestones, the Alia CTOL nailed one more, with a demonstration flight at the National Advanced Air Mobility Center of Excellence (NAAMCE), located at Springfield-Beckley Municipal Airport. The pilot was Kyle Clark himself, BETA founder and chief executive officer, and his passenger was Sinclair President and CEO Dr. Steven Johnson.\nThe flight demonstration came as the perfect way to seal a pioneering partnership for AAM (Advanced Air Mobility) and the world of academia. Sinclair Community College was the first academic institution of any kind in the US to purchase a BETA flight simulator, and will soon be the first to actually own an Alia electric aircraft.\nThis is all part of developing a complex training program that will give students first-hand access to an actual electric aircraft and the technical skills required to build a career in electric aviation. With eVTOLs and other types of electric aircraft closer to commercial service, there’s an obvious need for supporting training programs in higher education.\nA new generation of pilots, technicians, and maintenance personnel, is part of this new era of air mobility, and Sinclair is ready to be one of the first to support it, especially given Ohio’s strong aviation background, confirmed through the recent publication of an Advanced Air Mobility (AAM) Action Plan by the Ohio Department of Transportation (ODOT).\nBETA is not only the name behind the Alia eVTOL and CTOL, but also the only manufacturer in this industry that has also developed a certified electric aircraft charging system. With more than 160 multimodal charging sites currently active, BETA has an impressive electric charging network that keeps growing. These exact charging capabilities were also installed at NAAMCE in Springfield, in preparation for this demonstration flight and future flights.\nThe Alia CTOL has recently wrapped up its spectacular participation in the eIPP (it was selected to participate in seven of the total of eight programs), with Florida as the final state, where it has completed 14 flights, covering close to 700 nautical miles. Alia CTOL has also been selected by New Zealand’s Air New Zealand and Loganair in the UK for pioneering electric aviation operations.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nAlia CTOL beta technologies Alia eVTOL evtol electric aircraft\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Otilia Drăgan\nOtilia believes that if it’s eco, green, or groundbreaking, people should know about it (especially if it's got wheels or wings). Working in online media for over five years, she's gained a deeper perspective on how people everywhere can inspire each other. Full profile\nUP NEXT Houston Dealer Sells 2023 Porsche 718 Cayman GT4 RS Weissach for $211,000",
